libnginx-mod-http-coraza (0.23.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New upstream release 0.23.0.
  * Security: a bare `drop` action (no explicit status) and `deny,status:200`
    were not enforced: the request was served and the audit log recorded
    status 0. Every intervention now blocks; deny statuses below 300 are
    served as 403, `drop` closes the connection and is audited as 444.
  * After `rewrite ... last`, the rules of the location that finally handles
    the request apply (previously the first matched location's); subrequests
    answered in the rewrite phase are not inspected.
  * Phase-3 rules see the response headers as the client receives them
    (after gzip, charset and range): final Content-Encoding, Vary, charset,
    Server, and the Content-Length of a 206 slice. Response bodies are still
    inspected uncompressed.
  * A policy redirect replaces an existing Location header instead of
    adding a second one.
  * 204/304 and header-only responses (DAV PUT) are finalised in phase 4
    before their headers go out and are never delayed; a 304 carrying a
    Content-Type no longer leaks the file body.
  * SSI responses sent through the header delay no longer lose the sync
    flag on copied buffers (truncated non-chunked pages).
  * The request context is published only once fully constructed.
  * Phase 4 now runs for responses that never reach the body filter (HEAD,
    204, 304, and handler-marked header-only responses such as a WebDAV PUT)
    in both coraza_delay_response_headers modes; a phase-4 deny on ARGS or TX
    was silently skipped for them before.
  * On a 204, phase-3 rules no longer see the Content-Type, Content-Length
    and Last-Modified that nginx strips from the wire.
  * Build and CI: the obsolete Vagrant build.sh is gone; both fuzz targets
    run in the deep and memcheck jobs; soak failures keep their evidence;
    scanner gates fail on incomplete runs; valgrind suppressions cover the
    Go runtime's 1/2/4/16/32-byte accesses.

 -- Pierre Pomes <pierre.pomes@gmail.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0000

libnginx-mod-http-coraza (0.22.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * New upstream release 0.22.0.
  * Require libcoraza >= 1.8.0; support for 1.7 is dropped. The connector needs
    coraza_is_response_body_accessible(), new in 1.8, to know whether a
    response body will be inspected: configure fails against older headers
    and the worker refuses to start against an older shared library.
  * Response headers are no longer held back when the body will not be
    inspected (SecResponseBodyAccess Off, or a Content-Type outside
    SecResponseBodyMimeType): phase 4 is finalised before the headers go out,
    so streaming responses (chunked JSON, NDJSON, SSE) reach the client at
    once while phase-4 rules on non-body variables still deny cleanly.
  * SecRemoteRules is rejected at nginx -t instead of failing in every worker.
  * Fail-closed fixes: delayed-header responses are finalised instead of
    returning a bare status, premature response file EOF is rejected, a
    ruleless "coraza on" is rejected at startup, the request body is
    submitted to the engine exactly once, proxied headers are no longer
    submitted twice.
  * Performance: WAFs are deduplicated by rule content across locations,
    request body access off skips the body submission, request tempfiles are
    read in 64 KiB chunks, file-backed response inspection is bounded.

 -- Pierre Pomes <pierre.pomes@gmail.com>  Wed, 07 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

libnginx-mod-http-coraza (0.21.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * New upstream release 0.21.0.
  * Require libcoraza >= 1.7.0; support for older libcoraza is dropped. The
    connector gates on coraza_version_num() both at build time (configure
    fails against older headers) and at runtime (the worker refuses to start
    against an older shared library), and always uses the tri-state
    coraza_result_t return contract.

 -- Pierre Pomes <pierre.pomes@gmail.com>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000

libnginx-mod-http-coraza (0.20.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * New upstream release 0.20.0.
  * Security: fail closed when request-body, response-body, or body-phase
    processing fails in Coraza, and on NGX_ERROR from the intervention handler;
    always set the intervention flag when one fires; validate the transaction
    handle in create_ctx.
  * Bound worker memory: cap delayed response-body buffering, bound buffering of
    uninspected file-backed streams, and skip the header delay for SSE streams
    (upstream #81) and HEAD responses.
  * Correctness: report REQUEST_PROTOCOL/RESPONSE_PROTOCOL in slash-delimited
    form and report HTTP/3 to the WAF; sanitize synthesized redirect and
    HTTP/2/3 response headers; guard size_t-to-int narrowing at the cgo boundary.
  * Performance: submit request/response headers in a single cgo call with a
    per-header fallback, and cut per-request CGO crossings on the hot path;
    configurable response-header delay via coraza_delay_response_headers.
  * Build: require libcoraza 1.4 ABI, keep libcoraza loaded for the worker
    lifetime, and probe the libcoraza result-code contract at configure time.
  * The bulk of this release was contributed by Thijs Eilander (@eilandert).

 -- Pierre Pomes <pierre.pomes@gmail.com>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0000

